Aura Components (Legacy but still asked)

Even though Aura Components are largely replaced by LWC, they are still important for:

Interviews (very commonly asked)
Maintaining legacy Salesforce applications
Understanding evolution of UI frameworks in Salesforce
5.1 Basics
Component Structure

An Aura component is made up of multiple files bundled together.

7

Each component bundle typically contains:

File	Purpose
.cmp	Main component markup
.js	Client-side controller
Helper.js	Reusable JS logic
Renderer.js	Custom rendering (rarely used)
Design	For Lightning App Builder
Documentation	Description for developers
SVG	Icon
Example Component
<aura:component>
    <aura:attribute name="message" type="String" default="Hello Aura"/>

    <lightning:button label="Click Me" onclick="{!c.handleClick}"/>

    <p>{!v.message}</p>
</aura:component>
Attributes

Attributes are like state variables in Aura.

<aura:attribute name="count" type="Integer" default="0"/>
Key Points:
Defined using <aura:attribute>
Accessed using {!v.attributeName}
Can store:
Primitive types (String, Integer, Boolean)
Objects
Lists
5.2 Client-Side Controller

Aura uses JavaScript on the client side with Controller + Helper pattern.

JS Controller

Handles UI events.

({
    handleClick : function(component, event, helper) {
        component.set("v.message", "Button Clicked");
    }
})
Responsibilities:
Handle user interactions
Call helper functions
Trigger server-side calls
Helper

Contains reusable logic.

({
    updateMessage : function(component) {
        component.set("v.message", "Updated via Helper");
    }
})
Why Helper?
Avoid duplicate code
Keep controller clean
Share logic across functions
Flow
User Action → Controller → Helper → Update Component
5.3 Server-Side Calls (Apex Controller)

Aura interacts with Apex using actions.

Apex Controller
public with sharing class MyController {
    @AuraEnabled
    public static String getMessage() {
        return 'Hello from Apex';
    }
}
Calling Apex from Aura
({
    callApex : function(component) {
        var action = component.get("c.getMessage");

        action.setCallback(this, function(response) {
            var state = response.getState();
            if(state === "SUCCESS") {
                component.set("v.message", response.getReturnValue());
            }
        });

        $A.enqueueAction(action);
         }
})
Key Concepts
1. Action
Represents Apex method call
2. Callback
Handles async response
3. States
SUCCESS
ERROR
INCOMPLETE
Flow
Aura JS → Action → Apex → Response → Callback → UI Update
Important Interview Points
Aura is event-driven framework
Uses two-way data binding
Requires Controller + Helper pattern
Server calls are asynchronous
Replaced by LWC (better performance + standard JS)